          "why": "Squaring the speed gives 9, so the kinetic energy is 18 J.",
          "sol": "Step 1: KE = ½ × 4 × 3².\nStep 2: KE = 2 × 9.\nStep 3: KE = 18 J.",
          "hint": "First calculate 3².",
          "subject": "physics",
          "chapter": "work_energy_power",
          "topic": "Calculating Kinetic Energy",
          "conceptId": "physics_work_energy_power_calculating_kinetic_energy",
          "skillId": "physics_work_energy_power",
          "subskillId": "physics_work_energy_power_q2"
